-- title:
--   Orbit quotient $F/\langle\sigma\rangle$ with loops, $\hom(F/\langle\sigma\rangle,X)$, and $F/\langle\sigma\rangle\cong H$
-- statement:
--   Let $\sigma$ be an automorphism of a graph $F$ and $\langle\sigma\rangle$ the cyclic group of its powers, acting on $V(F)$. The **orbit quotient** $F/\langle\sigma\rangle$ has the vertex orbits of $\langle\sigma\rangle$ as its vertices; two orbits $O,O'$ are adjacent if $uv\in E(F)$ for some $u\in O$, $v\in O'$. Parallel quotient edges are merged, but **loops are retained**.
--
--   $\hom(F/\langle\sigma\rangle,X)$ counts the maps $\varphi$ from the orbits to $V(X)$ with $\varphi(O)\varphi(O')\in E(X)$ whenever $O,O'$ are adjacent; a loop in the quotient forces this count to be $0$. For a simple graph $H$, $F/\langle\sigma\rangle\cong H$ means that there is a bijection from the orbits onto $V(H)$ under which quotient adjacency corresponds exactly to adjacency in $H$ (in particular the quotient has no loops).
--
--   The quotient identity $\hom(F,X)\equiv\hom(F/\langle\sigma\rangle,X)\pmod p$ for automorphisms of prime order $p$ (Lemma 6) is the source of the modular congruences behind the counterexamples.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The orbits are `MulAction.orbitRel.Quotient (Subgroup.zpowers τ) V` for the underlying permutation `τ = σ.toEquiv`. A Mathlib `SimpleGraph` cannot carry loops, so the quotient is the symmetric relation `quotRel` on the orbit type, which may hold on the diagonal.
-- source:
--   Kristjánsson, Counterexamples to the Strong Roberson Conjecture, arXiv:2610.03550 (version of 2 November 2026, sha256 1b70a7c6…), §2.3, p.7 (PDF p.9); Corollary 7 p.8; Lemma 8 p.9

import Mathlib

set_option autoImplicit false

namespace RobersonCx

/-- The vertex orbits of the cyclic group `⟨τ⟩` generated by a permutation `τ` of `V` (§2.3, p.7 (PDF p.9)):
the vertices of the orbit quotient `F/⟨σ⟩` when `τ` is the underlying permutation of an automorphism `σ`. -/
abbrev OrbitQuot {V : Type*} (τ : Equiv.Perm V) : Type _ :=
  MulAction.orbitRel.Quotient (Subgroup.zpowers τ) V

/-- Adjacency of the orbit quotient `F/⟨τ⟩` (§2.3, p.7 (PDF p.9)): two orbits `O, O′` are adjacent if
`uv ∈ E(F)` for some `u ∈ O` and `v ∈ O′`. Parallel quotient edges are merged, but loops are retained.

**Formalization Note** A Mathlib `SimpleGraph` cannot carry loops, so the quotient is this symmetric relation on
the orbit type, which holds at `(O, O)` exactly when `F/⟨τ⟩` has a loop at `O`. -/
def quotRel {V : Type*} (F : SimpleGraph V) (τ : Equiv.Perm V) (O O' : OrbitQuot τ) : Prop :=
  ∃ u v : V, Quotient.mk _ u = O ∧ Quotient.mk _ v = O' ∧ F.Adj u v

/-- `hom(F/⟨τ⟩, X)`: the number of maps `φ` from the orbits to `V(X)` with `φ(O)φ(O′) ∈ E(X)` whenever `O, O′`
are adjacent in `F/⟨τ⟩` (§2.1 and §2.3, pp.5, 7).

**Formalization Note** A loop at `O` in the quotient forces `X.Adj (φ O) (φ O)`, which never holds in a simple
graph, so a quotient with a loop has `hom = 0`, as in the paper. -/
noncomputable def homCountQuot {V W : Type*} (F : SimpleGraph V) (τ : Equiv.Perm V) (X : SimpleGraph W) : ℕ :=
  Nat.card {φ : OrbitQuot τ → W // ∀ O O' : OrbitQuot τ, quotRel F τ O O' → X.Adj (φ O) (φ O')}

/-- `F/⟨τ⟩ ≅ H` for a simple graph `H` (Corollary 7, p.8 and Lemma 8, p.9): a bijection `e` from the orbits
onto `V(H)` with `O, O′` adjacent in the quotient iff `e O, e O′` are adjacent in `H`.

**Formalization Note** Since `H` has no loops, such an `e` exists only if the quotient is loopless. -/
def QuotIsoTo {V U : Type*} (F : SimpleGraph V) (τ : Equiv.Perm V) (H : SimpleGraph U) : Prop :=
  ∃ e : OrbitQuot τ ≃ U, ∀ O O' : OrbitQuot τ, quotRel F τ O O' ↔ H.Adj (e O) (e O')

end RobersonCx


